<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Casimir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as New Amstel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Casimir was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fort built in 1651 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a> to challenge the colony of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. The Swedes seized it in 1654 and renamed it Fort Trinity, prompting the Dutch to conquer all of New Sweden in 1655. Renamed New Amstel, it later passed to the English and became the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-castle-delaware">New Castle, Delaware</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-casimir.jpg" alt="Fort Casimir" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dedden 11:31, 30 April 2006 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nieuw_Nederland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> called the Delaware the South River and regarded the lands along it and its bay as the southern flank of New Netherland. From 1638 Swedes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a>, Dutch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloons">Walloons</a> settled the valley as the colony of New Sweden, which the Dutch never officially recognised. Fort Casimir stood on a barrier island that no longer exists, at the end of Chestnut Street in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-castle-delaware">New Castle, Delaware</a>, on the western bank downstream from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-christina">Fort Christina</a>, one of the largest Swedish settlements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a>, Director-General of New Netherland from 1647, first built Fort Beversreede at the mouth of the Schuylkill, near the end of the Great Minquas Path, since Fort Nassau on the eastern bank lay far from the richest fur country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>. In 1651 he had that fort dismantled and moved to the new site, which he named Fort Casimir. On Trinity Sunday in 1654 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-risingh">Johan Risingh</a> took up office in New Sweden, set out to expel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and received the fort's surrender, renaming it Fort Trinity.</p><p>Stuyvesant retook it on 11 September 1655 and named it New Amstel. Fort Christina fell on 15 September and New Sweden passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, with New Amstel, under its vice director John Paul Jacquet, as their regional stronghold beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>. It has been suggested that the Peach War attack was retaliation by native peoples who saw their treaty with the Swedes as a defence alliance. In 1664 the English took New Netherland; the fort offered some resistance but soon fell. It was Dutch again in 1673 and 1674 and returned to the English after the Treaty of Westminster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fort was abandoned around 1675 and its site put to various commercial uses, with low ground around it filled with ash. A parking lot and several houses are believed to stand over its remains, and a preliminary archaeological survey was made in 1986. The name may honour the Polish king John Casimir, Stuyvesant's contemporary; the suggestion that it honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-casimir-i-count-of-nassau-dietz">Ernst Casimir of Nassau-Dietz</a> has no documentary support.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Casimir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch built <b>Fort Casimir</b> in 1651, lost it to the Swedes in 1654, and in 1655 Stuyvesant led the expedition that conquered New Sweden, renaming its chief settlement New Amstel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>New Netherland conquered the Swedish colony in 1655, and its <b>Fort Casimir</b>, renamed New Amstel, later became New Castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/delaware-colony">Delaware Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1654 Governor Johan Risingh seized the Dutch <b>Fort Casimir</b>, provoking a response.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-sweden">New Sweden</a></li></ul></section>
